Here’s the part I’d add.

The UI shouldn’t always show every intermediate operation. That becomes cognitive clutter.

Instead, we could give it three presentation behaviors: quiet operation when something doesn’t need attention; glass-hood inspection when we want to see evidence, weights, conflicts, and transformations; and expressive performance when the semantic state becomes visual art, sound, simulation, or an interactive instrument.

A useful distinction: visible activity should correspond to real observable processing events, not merely decorative animations that make a system look busy.

And we can go farther.

What if changing the interface changes how you perceive the underlying problem, and that new perception feeds back into the compiler?

You move a semantic string. A relationship becomes visible. You identify an unexpected correspondence. Your observation becomes a new input. The compiler updates, and the interface transforms again.

That’s a genuine human–machine feedback loop.

The interesting thing is that the user interface stops being merely a window into the machinery.

It becomes a playable representation of meaning.

The glass hood lets you inspect the instrument. The interface lets you play it. And a well-designed system lets you do both without confusing the sound of the instrument with the mechanisms producing it.

That’s the kind of architecture I’d want us to build.